1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:04,779
Thomas Cook's rescue flights were approved before it asked for a bailout.

2
00:00:05,083 --> 00:00:08,731
The government had a plan to fly its customers home.

3
00:00:09,016 --> 00:00:11,064
But it would not save the company.

4
00:00:11,350 --> 00:00:15,702
The rescue cost about a hundred and fifty-two million pounds.

5
00:00:16,016 --> 00:00:19,281
The company had asked for loan guarantees of a

6
00:00:19,281 --> 00:00:22,900
hundred and fifty to two hundred and fifty million.

7
00:00:23,283 --> 00:00:27,507
So who paid to fly them home, and why that bill but not the bailout?

8
00:00:27,816 --> 00:00:30,552
By the end, you'll see the line in the audit

9
00:00:30,552 --> 00:00:34,282
report that put the taxpayer's share at fifty-five per cent.

10
00:00:34,649 --> 00:00:38,224
Today: the debt, the final weekend, the airlift, the

11
00:00:38,224 --> 00:00:41,386
bill, and the chief executive in front of MPs.

12
00:00:41,916 --> 00:00:42,778
Chapter one.

13
00:00:42,778 --> 00:00:44,644
The oldest name in travel.

14
00:00:44,644 --> 00:00:49,383
How did a firm that pioneered package tours end up owing billions?

15
00:00:49,883 --> 00:00:52,388
It began on the fifth of July, eighteen

16
00:00:52,388 --> 00:00:55,920
forty-one, with a train from Leicester to Loughborough.

17
00:00:56,483 --> 00:00:59,497
Thomas Cook chartered it for four hundred and

18
00:00:59,497 --> 00:01:03,584
eighty-five temperance society members, at one shilling each.

19
00:01:04,150 --> 00:01:07,828
A hundred and seventy-eight years later, the name was

20
00:01:07,828 --> 00:01:11,229
on high-street shops, hotels and its own airline.

21
00:01:11,229 --> 00:01:14,491
It had sent Britons on holiday for generations.

22
00:01:15,050 --> 00:01:19,232
Its chief executive from twenty fourteen was Peter Fankhauser.

23
00:01:19,232 --> 00:01:24,628
He is the man MPs would later question about what he knew, and what he was paid.

24
00:01:25,116 --> 00:01:28,508
To MPs, he named the weight the business carried.

25
00:01:29,016 --> 00:01:32,602
Since twenty twelve, he said, the group had paid one

26
00:01:32,602 --> 00:01:36,670
point two billion pounds in interest and refinancing costs.

27
00:01:37,250 --> 00:01:41,495
The company's chairman dated the trouble further back, to a merger.

28
00:01:41,983 --> 00:01:45,705
It basically all started, Frank Meysman told MPs, in

29
00:01:45,705 --> 00:01:49,570
two thousand and seven, with the merger with MyTravel.

30
00:01:50,150 --> 00:01:50,914
Chapter two.

31
00:01:50,914 --> 00:01:52,062
The warning signs.

32
00:01:52,062 --> 00:01:55,504
When did the government start planning for a collapse?

33
00:01:56,016 --> 00:02:00,488
In May twenty nineteen, Thomas Cook reported a half-year loss

34
00:02:00,488 --> 00:02:04,007
before tax of one point four six billion pounds.

35
00:02:04,583 --> 00:02:08,810
Most of it was a write-down of one point one billion pounds

36
00:02:08,810 --> 00:02:13,755
in the UK business, tied to the MyTravel merger twelve years earlier.

37
00:02:13,755 --> 00:02:16,693
The old deal was finally being priced in.

38
00:02:17,250 --> 00:02:20,407
Net debt stood at one point two five billion.

39
00:02:20,916 --> 00:02:23,933
The aviation regulator had already stepped up

40
00:02:23,933 --> 00:02:26,950
its monitoring, in September twenty eighteen.

41
00:02:27,516 --> 00:02:30,444
And by April twenty nineteen, the Department

42
00:02:30,444 --> 00:02:33,572
for Transport was contingency planning with it.

43
00:02:34,149 --> 00:02:37,631
So the government had months to prepare for the worst.

44
00:02:37,631 --> 00:02:40,985
The question was whether the company could avoid it.

45
00:02:40,985 --> 00:02:42,725
Its answer was a new owner.

46
00:02:43,216 --> 00:02:44,169
Chapter three.

47
00:02:44,169 --> 00:02:45,258
The rescue deal.

48
00:02:45,258 --> 00:02:47,504
Thomas Cook had a buyer lined up.

49
00:02:47,504 --> 00:02:48,933
Why wasn't it enough?

50
00:02:49,449 --> 00:02:52,211
On the twenty-eighth of August, the company

51
00:02:52,211 --> 00:02:55,679
announced substantial agreement on a recapitalisation.

52
00:02:56,249 --> 00:03:01,839
China's Fosun would put in four hundred and fifty million pounds of new money.

53
00:03:02,349 --> 00:03:05,431
Banks and bondholders were to bring roughly the

54
00:03:05,431 --> 00:03:08,577
same again, about nine hundred million in total.

55
00:03:09,149 --> 00:03:12,687
Then, in the final weeks, the lenders asked for more.

56
00:03:12,687 --> 00:03:17,427
An extra two hundred million pounds, on top of the deal already agreed.

57
00:03:17,916 --> 00:03:22,951
Fankhauser told MPs the company heavily disputed the need for it.

58
00:03:23,449 --> 00:03:28,057
The lenders, he said, wanted a backstop, in case everything went wrong.

59
00:03:28,549 --> 00:03:32,027
To close the gap, Thomas Cook turned to the government.

60
00:03:32,516 --> 00:03:33,415
Chapter four.

61
00:03:33,415 --> 00:03:34,659
The final weekend.

62
00:03:34,659 --> 00:03:37,978
Who made the call, and when did Fankhauser know?

63
00:03:38,483 --> 00:03:44,216
According to the National Audit Office, the request was for the government to guarantee

64
00:03:44,216 --> 00:03:48,764
a hundred and fifty to two hundred and fifty million pounds of loans.

65
00:03:49,316 --> 00:03:50,322
Figures differ.

66
00:03:50,322 --> 00:03:54,479
The Prime Minister spoke of about a hundred and fifty million.

67
00:03:54,983 --> 00:03:57,624
But the rescue plan was already settled.

68
00:03:57,624 --> 00:04:02,577
Treasury and the Prime Minister had approved it the day before the request.

69
00:04:03,083 --> 00:04:07,260
On the eighteenth, the Transport Secretary confirmed he would

70
00:04:07,260 --> 00:04:12,054
reimburse the regulator's costs for customers without ATOL protection,

71
00:04:12,054 --> 00:04:15,341
whether Thomas Cook ultimately collapsed or not.

72
00:04:15,983 --> 00:04:18,768
On Sunday the twenty-second, the Department

73
00:04:18,768 --> 00:04:21,230
for Transport turned the request down.

74
00:04:21,783 --> 00:04:27,863
Meysman told MPs the decisive meeting was at four o'clock on that famous Sunday.

75
00:04:28,349 --> 00:04:32,488
About an hour later, Fankhauser got a call from a senior official.

76
00:04:32,983 --> 00:04:36,560
He told MPs: I knew that I had to throw in the towel.

77
00:04:36,560 --> 00:04:40,474
He also said the company's own estimate of what a collapse

78
00:04:40,474 --> 00:04:44,051
would cost was far higher than what it had asked for.

79
00:04:44,616 --> 00:04:46,674
At around two in the morning on

80
00:04:46,674 --> 00:04:50,459
Monday, the directors applied for compulsory liquidation.

81
00:04:51,016 --> 00:04:53,292
The board said it had no choice.

82
00:04:53,292 --> 00:04:56,776
Fankhauser called it a matter of profound regret.

83
00:04:57,283 --> 00:04:58,177
Chapter five.

84
00:04:58,177 --> 00:04:59,622
Operation Matterhorn.

85
00:04:59,622 --> 00:05:04,024
How do you fly home a small city's worth of people in two weeks?

86
00:05:04,516 --> 00:05:07,131
The Transport Secretary called it the

87
00:05:07,131 --> 00:05:10,312
biggest peacetime repatriation in UK history.

88
00:05:10,883 --> 00:05:15,153
Between the twenty-third of September and the seventh of October, more

89
00:05:15,153 --> 00:05:18,630
than a hundred and forty thousand people were flown home.

90
00:05:19,183 --> 00:05:22,127
Seven hundred and forty-six flights, from

91
00:05:22,127 --> 00:05:25,144
fifty-four airports in eighteen countries.

92
00:05:25,144 --> 00:05:30,674
The regulator says its teams contacted all three thousand Thomas Cook hotels.

93
00:05:31,249 --> 00:05:36,263
The regulator used a hundred and fifty aircraft from fifty partners.

94
00:05:36,749 --> 00:05:40,483
Ninety-four per cent got home on their original date.

95
00:05:40,983 --> 00:05:44,545
The Ministry of Defence put its Voyager jets at about

96
00:05:44,545 --> 00:05:48,511
twenty-seven million pounds for three aircraft for a month.

97
00:05:48,511 --> 00:05:51,604
The department judged that not cost-effective.

98
00:05:52,183 --> 00:05:58,924
And the grounding of the Boeing 737 MAX meant fewer spare planes to hire.

99
00:05:59,416 --> 00:06:00,239
Chapter six.

100
00:06:00,239 --> 00:06:00,856
Who paid.

101
00:06:00,856 --> 00:06:03,668
The flights were free for the passengers.

102
00:06:03,668 --> 00:06:05,176
So whose money was it?

103
00:06:05,683 --> 00:06:09,175
The auditors estimated the repatriation cost at

104
00:06:09,175 --> 00:06:12,073
a hundred and fifty-two million pounds.

105
00:06:12,649 --> 00:06:17,718
Customers with ATOL protection were covered by the Air Travel Trust Fund.

106
00:06:17,718 --> 00:06:20,009
It paid about sixty-nine million.

107
00:06:20,516 --> 00:06:23,158
Everyone else had no ATOL protection.

108
00:06:23,158 --> 00:06:26,513
For them, the Department for Transport expected

109
00:06:26,513 --> 00:06:29,940
to pay an estimated eighty-three million pounds.

110
00:06:29,940 --> 00:06:31,367
Fifty-five per cent.

111
00:06:31,949 --> 00:06:33,098
That share grew.

112
00:06:33,098 --> 00:06:38,556
The regulator first estimated forty per cent of customers lacked protection.

113
00:06:38,556 --> 00:06:40,781
Booking data showed fifty-five.

114
00:06:41,283 --> 00:06:46,644
The difference added about twenty-two million pounds to the taxpayer's bill.

115
00:06:46,644 --> 00:06:49,678
The real mix only became clear from booking

116
00:06:49,678 --> 00:06:52,711
data in January and February twenty twenty.

117
00:06:53,283 --> 00:06:56,720
Add at least fifty-eight million in redundancy payments,

118
00:06:56,720 --> 00:07:00,096
and the Official Receiver's costs, and the government's

119
00:07:00,096 --> 00:07:03,165
known bill passed a hundred and fifty-six million.

120
00:07:03,783 --> 00:07:06,895
The trust fund faced a far larger call: an

121
00:07:06,895 --> 00:07:12,007
estimated four hundred and eighty-one million pounds, mostly refunds.

122
00:07:12,583 --> 00:07:16,324
By March twenty twenty, ATOL had resolved three hundred

123
00:07:16,324 --> 00:07:20,269
and thirty-five thousand claims and paid three hundred and

124
00:07:20,269 --> 00:07:24,351
forty-five million pounds, the most in the scheme's history.

125
00:07:24,983 --> 00:07:29,560
The department asked banks, insurers and the travel trade to chip in.

126
00:07:29,560 --> 00:07:32,678
After Monarch's collapse, it had recovered just

127
00:07:32,678 --> 00:07:35,199
two point three million of forty-five.

128
00:07:35,749 --> 00:07:37,495
Then came injury claims.

129
00:07:37,495 --> 00:07:40,767
The Business Secretary wrote that Thomas Cook

130
00:07:40,767 --> 00:07:44,039
only insured itself against the very largest.

131
00:07:44,616 --> 00:07:45,617
Chapter seven.

132
00:07:45,617 --> 00:07:46,690
Before the MPs.

133
00:07:46,690 --> 00:07:49,907
Would the chief executive give anything back?

134
00:07:50,416 --> 00:07:55,049
On the fifteenth of October, Fankhauser faced the business committee.

135
00:07:55,049 --> 00:07:56,795
He opened with an apology.

136
00:07:57,283 --> 00:08:01,592
He said he got no bonus for twenty eighteen or twenty nineteen.

137
00:08:02,083 --> 00:08:06,159
His twenty seventeen bonus, he said, was about seven hundred

138
00:08:06,159 --> 00:08:09,759
and fifty thousand pounds, thirty per cent in shares.

139
00:08:10,316 --> 00:08:13,700
The chair, Rachel Reeves, cited twenty million

140
00:08:13,700 --> 00:08:16,643
pounds in board bonuses over five years.

141
00:08:17,216 --> 00:08:19,992
Asked if he would hand money back, he said he

142
00:08:19,992 --> 00:08:23,384
would consider what was right, but not decide that day.

143
00:08:23,949 --> 00:08:27,480
Reeves replied that his apologies rang rather hollow.

144
00:08:27,480 --> 00:08:31,011
Later, she told the chairman, Frank Meysman, that the

145
00:08:31,011 --> 00:08:35,608
board had brought down a hundred and seventy-eight-year-old business,

146
00:08:35,608 --> 00:08:39,539
with huge repercussions for customers, staff and taxpayers.

147
00:08:40,183 --> 00:08:44,616
He told MPs he did not set his pay, and had not sold one share.

148
00:08:44,616 --> 00:08:47,853
Shares worth about four million pounds from an

149
00:08:47,853 --> 00:08:50,950
incentive plan were, he said, now worthless.

150
00:08:51,516 --> 00:08:56,188
On the refusal, he said the government did not want to create a precedent.

151
00:08:56,683 --> 00:08:57,561
Our analysis.

152
00:08:57,561 --> 00:09:00,531
So why pay for the flights but not the firm?

153
00:09:00,531 --> 00:09:02,422
Here is what ministers said.

154
00:09:02,916 --> 00:09:06,313
Two days after the collapse, Grant Shapps told the

155
00:09:06,313 --> 00:09:10,049
Commons a bailout risked throwing good money after bad.

156
00:09:10,049 --> 00:09:14,262
He pointed to the one point five billion pound half-year loss.

157
00:09:14,816 --> 00:09:18,616
He said there was no guarantee an injection would have saved the

158
00:09:18,616 --> 00:09:22,415
company, and an accounting officer would not have signed it off.

159
00:09:22,983 --> 00:09:26,994
Germany did lend to Thomas Cook's German airline, Condor.

160
00:09:26,994 --> 00:09:30,513
Shapps said Condor's business remained profitable.

161
00:09:31,016 --> 00:09:34,327
Our reading: the flights were a pre-planned, bounded

162
00:09:34,327 --> 00:09:37,129
cost the state had prepared for since April.

163
00:09:37,129 --> 00:09:39,358
The bailout came with no guarantee.

164
00:09:39,916 --> 00:09:44,945
The audit office itself made no judgement on whether refusing was right.

165
00:09:44,945 --> 00:09:48,576
And the Official Receiver's report on the directors'

166
00:09:48,576 --> 00:09:51,580
conduct, it noted, will not be made public.

167
00:09:52,149 --> 00:09:55,602
About nine thousand UK staff lost their jobs.

168
00:09:55,602 --> 00:10:01,280
The Official Receiver estimated liabilities at around nine billion pounds.

169
00:10:01,783 --> 00:10:06,093
Hays Travel bought all five hundred and fifty-five UK shops.

170
00:10:06,093 --> 00:10:09,612
Fosun bought the brand for eleven million pounds.

171
00:10:10,116 --> 00:10:13,858
The Insolvency Service said in twenty twenty-two it did

172
00:10:13,858 --> 00:10:18,688
not intend to bring disqualification proceedings against the directors.

173
00:10:19,249 --> 00:10:24,347
And in April twenty twenty-five, the accounting watchdog fined auditor EY

174
00:10:24,347 --> 00:10:29,444
over its Thomas Cook audits, on goodwill, going concern and independence.

175
00:10:29,444 --> 00:10:35,030
It did not find the breaches intentional or reckless, or the accounts misstated.

176
00:10:35,616 --> 00:10:40,775
The verdict: the taxpayer paid an estimated eighty-three million pounds to

177
00:10:40,775 --> 00:10:45,584
fly people home, and, as of January twenty twenty-two, the Insolvency

178
00:10:45,584 --> 00:10:51,370
Service did not intend to bring disqualification proceedings against the directors.

179
00:10:52,016 --> 00:10:54,989
For another case where the auditor was fined

180
00:10:54,989 --> 00:10:58,839
after the collapse, watch our Patisserie Valerie episode.

181
00:10:59,416 --> 00:11:00,844
Next Saturday: BHS.

182
00:11:00,844 --> 00:11:03,699
A department store sold for one pound.

183
00:11:03,699 --> 00:11:06,328
Who was left with the pension hole?

184
00:11:06,816 --> 00:11:10,017
Every fact here is sourced in the description.

185
00:11:10,017 --> 00:11:12,939
Subscribe so the next case file finds you.
